<section>
    <h2>Frames: a page inside the page</h2>
    <p>
        An <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> is a whole separate document: a payment form, an embedded editor, a widget
        from another team. Page-level locators only search the page's own document, so they never find
        anything inside a frame.
    </p>
<pre><code>// Finds nothing: the button lives in the frame's document.
await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Pay" }).ClickAsync();

// Scope the search to the frame first.
var payment = Page.FrameLocator("#payment");
await payment.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Pay" }).ClickAsync();</code></pre>
    <p>
        <code>FrameLocator</code> takes a selector for the <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> element, and everything you
        chain after it searches inside that frame. It waits for the frame to load, like any other locator.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Shadow DOM: Playwright looks inside for you</h2>
    <p>
        Web components hide their insides in a <em>shadow root</em>, so the page's styles and scripts can't
        reach in by accident. In some tools you have to dig through <code>shadowRoot</code> by hand.
        In Playwright you don't: role, text, label and CSS locators all look inside open shadow roots.
    </p>
<pre><code>// The button is inside &lt;magic-widget&gt;'s shadow root. No extra steps needed.
await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Open" }).ClickAsync();</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>The exception:</strong> XPath doesn't go into shadow roots. One more reason to prefer
        user-facing locators.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Dialogs: say yes before you're asked</h2>
    <p>
        <code>alert()</code>, <code>confirm()</code> and <code>prompt()</code> open native browser dialogs. Nobody is
        there to click OK during a test, so Playwright <strong>dismisses</strong> them: <code>confirm()</code>
        returns <code>false</code>, and the page carries on as if the user clicked Cancel. The test doesn't fail.
        The page just quietly doesn't do the thing.
    </p>
    <p>To accept instead, register a handler <em>before</em> the action that opens the dialog:</p>
<pre><code>Page.Dialog += async (_, dialog) =>
{
    TestContext.Out.WriteLine($"Dialog said: {dialog.Message}");
    await dialog.AcceptAsync();
};

await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Delete" }).ClickAsync();</code></pre>
    <p>
        The handler runs for every dialog from then on, including ones opened inside frames. For a
        <code>prompt()</code>, pass the text to type: <code>dialog.AcceptAsync("my answer")</code>.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>All three at once</h2>
    <p>
        In the trap room there's a mirror. It's in a frame. What's in the frame is in a shadow root. And breaking
        it asks if you're sure. You have eight seconds, and your reflection is watching.
    </p>
</section>
